@brief
The error code `REFUND_FAILED` is being renamed `PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED`.
It lives in the service that throws it, the test that asserts on it,
and the error table `default.json`, which is not a TypeScript file, so
`**/*.ts` would miss it. It also appears in `docs/CHANGELOG.md`, in the
entry for the release that introduced it. A changelog is history and
does not get rewritten.

`:grep` hands the search to the external `grep` program and reads the
results back into the same quickfix list. It is faster than `:vimgrep`
on a big tree, because it is grep, and it takes grep's arguments, not
vim's: the pattern is a grep regex, and what follows is whatever you
would type after `grep -n` in a shell. `:grep -r REFUND_FAILED src`
searches everything under `src`, every file type, and leaves `docs`
alone because you did not name it.

Rename the code in the three files under `src`. If vim pauses on the
grep output and asks you to press Enter, press it.

@keys
| `:grep args` | run the system grep, results into the quickfix list |
| `:grep -r pattern dir` | recursive, under `dir` |
| `:cdo` `:copen` `:cn` | all work the same on a `:grep` list |

@hints
`:grep -r REFUND_FAILED src`. Three hits, in whatever order grep found
them.
--
It is the same list. `:cdo s/REFUND_FAILED/PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED/g | update`.
--
`:q`.

@solution
    :grep -r REFUND_FAILED src<Enter>  :cdo s/REFUND_FAILED/PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED/g | update<Enter>  :q<Enter>

`:vimgrep` reads every candidate file into vim and runs vim's own
regex over it: exact vim syntax, `ignorecase` and `smartcase` honoured,
slow on a large tree. `:grep` runs a separate program: fast, its own
regex dialect, its own case rules (`-i` if you want them ignored), and
its own idea of which files to look at. On a repository of a few
thousand files the difference is seconds against a blink. Once the
results are in the list, nothing downstream cares which command
produced them.

Two things to carry. The directory argument is your filter: `src`, not
`.`, or the changelog and everything else at the root comes along. And
grep returns hits in the order it walked the disk, not alphabetically,
so do not plan a `:cn` route around the order you expect; for a `:cdo`
the order never matters.

`--include='*.ts'` gives grep a file-type filter when you need one. The
next lesson bakes it in.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.

@start docs/CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 1.4.0

- Refunds now go through the payment gateway. A rejected refund raises
  `REFUND_FAILED` with a 502.

## 1.3.2

- Fix: cancelled orders were refunded twice when the ledger retried.

